 "about": "Two parallel ranges: the C15 nave and chancel (north, 9 squares wide) and the original nave, now the south aisle, with the Norman central tower and the former chancel, now the south chapel (south, 6 wide); a south porch and a vestry; galleries at both west ends; a C19 broach spire. Liturgical east is map north (y 0); the south side faces map east (x 15/20).",
 "evidence": [
  "List entry 1338903 (OGL): 'Mainly C15, but base of the tower Norman. C19 spire. Original nave now the south aisle. Jacobean pulpit.'",
  "VCH Hampshire II plan (1903, looked at only): the two ranges, the central tower in the south range with round-arched openings W, E and N, the S chapel (former chancel), the porch and vestry on the south, galleries at both west ends.",
  "Photos (Commons, looked at only): flint walls with stone dressings and Perpendicular windows; the tower pale and rendered with a blue clock dial and louvred belfry openings; a broach spire of grey shingles with a weathercock; slate roofs; inside, white walls, a plain round Saxon tub font and a panelled C15 font.",
  "VCH plan (looked at only): the north chapel projects north of the chancel, about 5 squares deep and 7 long (map squares 616-620, 738-744: map north is real west here); neither OSM nor the LiDAR outline has it, so `extend` adds it. The round stair turret stands at the tower's south-west corner.",
  "The north door (0, 22) is the church's real north door: no path reaches that side on the map; Build's yards.py lays one to it.",
  "Doors where the map's paths meet the walls (landmark.py brief): the west door at x 7, the porch's at y 25, the vestry's on its east side.",
  "Build's shell (LiDAR) read the two ranges as 4-storey lean-tos and the tower as a flat 10 storeys: the roof is redrawn here as two gabled ranges of 2 storeys (eaves about 6 m, as the photos show), the tower 6 storeys (18 m) under a 15 m spire."
